      "diagnosis": "The task is explicitly an empty-string JSON key (\"\") deserialization loophole; the produced mitigation (annotation tweaks) did not change exploit behavior, indicating the skill content does not adequately translate the empty-key concept into actionable remediation steps. It under-specifies how empty keys can be accepted and routed (e.g., permissive object-node/Map binding, @JsonAnySetter, creator-based binding), why annotation-only approaches can fail, and what concrete pre-deserialization or mapper-level protections to apply and how to test them.",
      "repair_instruction": "Augment the skill with an operational remediation workflow for empty-key/unknown-property vectors: (1) explain the concrete routing mechanisms for empty field names in Jackson (Map binding, @JsonAnySetter, JsonNode/ObjectNode to POJO conversion, creator properties) and how they can bypass injected-value expectations; (2) prescribe rejecting field names == \"\" before binding using a streaming parser pass or a Jackson hook (custom JsonFactory/JsonParser decorator, DeserializationProblemHandler, or module that errors on empty currentName), with code snippets; (3) add strict ObjectMapper configuration guidance where applicable (e.g., FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES, limiting permissive any-setter patterns, avoiding broad JsonNode->POJO conversions for security-sensitive configs); (4) include a validation/testing checklist tailored to CVE-2021-25646-style payloads: confirm the exact deserialization entry point used by the endpoint, ensure exploit returns 4xx with explicit error, and ensure legitimate sampler requests still succeed."
