      "diagnosis": "Part of the failure is likely applying the mitigation at the wrong layer or not affecting the ObjectMapper/deserialization path actually exercised by the exploit endpoint, a common issue in large Java services. The current skill package is broad, but it lacks targeted guidance for locating and hardening the specific HTTP/Jackson binding path in a multi-module system like Druid, and for scoping a fix to one request type without unintended breakage.",
      "repair_instruction": "Add a task-focused section on implementing and verifying Jackson hardening in real services: (1) how to trace which endpoint/controller/resource method is hit and which ObjectMapper (or json mapper utility) is used on that code path; (2) how to apply endpoint-scoped vs global Jackson changes (custom Module/DeserializationProblemHandler/BeanDeserializerModifier vs per-reader ObjectReader) and choose the least risky scope; (3) patterns for security-critical DTO validation at the boundary (reject malformed keys/unknown structures) while maintaining backward compatibility; (4) an integration-test recipe that asserts the exploit payload is blocked (4xx) and baseline functionality remains (200)."
